####################################################################
# cardiac_arrest.rq — clinical event CardiacArrest. HAND-AUTHORED.
#
# Constituent event for CQ10 (manuscript Table 1): can coinciding alarms be identified that
# together form a different clinical meaning than their independent meaning,
# and for which no individual alarm exists?
#
# Criterion (FRAMEWORK/KNOWLEDGE_BASE/clinicalEvents.ttl, revised 2026-11-25):
#   evidencedBy some (metric:CardiacRhythm and
#                     (hasRhythm value Absent or hasRhythm value Fibrillation))
# — a cardiac-rhythm metric with an absent rhythm (asystole) or fibrillation
# (ventricular fibrillation refines Fibrillation), reported by an alarm of
# this patient that is still active and not CAT1-flagged. The metric's type
# follows rdfs:subClassOf* and the rhythm mda:refinesConcept* (the property
# chain the ontology declares on hasRhythm; the POC does not materialise
# it), so a narrower concept counts without naming it here.
# SAFETY EXTENSION (agreed 2026-11-25), beyond the KB axiom: an alarm that
# reports EXACTLY "VF or VT" (rhythm VentricularFibrillationOrTachycardia,
# "PHILIPSMONITOR - Ventrikel Fibr/Tachy") also counts — it cannot tell the
# two apart, so it is treated as a possible VF. Matched exactly, NOT through
# refinesConcept*: VentricularTachycardia refines "VF or VT", and a pure VT
# alarm (can have a pulse) must not count. For the same reason the KB axiom
# cannot carry this: through the hasRhythm property chain, a VT report
# entails hasRhythm "VF or VT", so a hasValue restriction on it would make
# every VT a cardiac arrest. Not Abnormal ("Hoge ARR"). An
# alarm CAT1 flagged as likely false positive supports no clinical event
# (agreed 2026-09-21): a condition must not rest on an alarm the POC itself
# believes to be false. A withdrawn CAT1b flag lets the asystole count from
# the withdrawal on.
#
# GRAPHS (contract: structure persists, states are transient). ?gEv is one
# alarm's transient graph: its message (hence its patient), the STATE it
# reports (hasRhythm Absent) and any CAT1 flag on it. A state found there
# is that alarm's report, on that alarm's metric. The metric's TYPE is
# structure, read from the same alarm's own persistent graph ?gEvP, so no
# hop is borrowed from another alarm. The state anchors the match: the
# transient graph is dropped when the alarm ends, so the condition stops
# holding then (episode_end.rq), although the metric node itself stays in
# ?gEvP for the post-alarm window.
#
# CONTRACT. Bound on entry: ?patient, ?now. Binds: ?evidence (the metric),
# ?support (the alarm's graph). Used by the episode actions
# (actions/episode_*.rq), which maintain one event per episode.
####################################################################

PREFIX mda:    <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#>
PREFIX mdapoc: <https://w3id.org/mda/poc#>
PREFIX rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#>
PREFIX rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
PREFIX metric: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/metric/>
PREFIX rhythm: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/metric-rhythm/>

GRAPH ?gEv { ?evAlarm mda:hasMessage ?evMsg . ?evMsg mda:concernsPatient ?patient .
             ?evidence mda:hasRhythm ?rhythm . }
{ ?rhythm mda:refinesConcept* ?arrestRhythm .
  VALUES ?arrestRhythm { rhythm:Absent rhythm:Fibrillation } }
UNION
{ VALUES ?rhythm { rhythm:VentricularFibrillationOrTachycardia } }
BIND(IRI(CONCAT(STR(?evAlarm), "#persistent")) AS ?gEvP)
GRAPH ?gEvP { ?evidence rdf:type ?metricType . }
?metricType rdfs:subClassOf* metric:CardiacRhythm .
FILTER NOT EXISTS { GRAPH ?gEv { ?evAlarm mdapoc:flaggedLikelyFalsePositive ?why } }
BIND(?gEv AS ?support)
